# "A Common Pride and Fame": The Attack and Repulse of Pickett's Division, July 3, 1863, Part I (NPS study; typescript, light OCR noise).
# Passages that are the study author's own words are marked as such; passages that quote a soldier's account say so in the claim (the account is a 1863-1900s recollection quoted by the study).
@ NPS-COMMON-PRIDE-1
%basis soldiers' accounts and maps as cited in the study's footnotes (OR, regimental histories, the Warren map)
24 | fences | Gibbon's line July 2-3: earth thrown over the existing stone wall, a post-and-rail fence on top in places | P-01,E-17,FEN-cemetery-ridge-walls | 1863-07-03 | stated | The study says Gibbon's Division, posted since the morning of July 2, strengthened its positions by throwing earth over the existing stone wall, which in places was surmounted by a common post-and-rail fence | =This division, posted since the morning of the 2nd, had strengthened their positions where possible by throwing earth over the existing stone wall. In places this wall was surmounted by “a common post-and-rail fence”.
27 | fences | 20th Massachusetts behind a slight earth breastwork against a low stone fence topped with rails | P-01,E-17 | 1863-07-03 | stated | The study says the Twentieth Massachusetts lay in the first line behind a slight breastwork made by throwing earth against a low stone fence topped with rails | =the Twentieth Massachusetts was directly in front in the first line, lying behind a slight breastwork made by throwing some earth up against a low stone fence, topped with rails.
87 | earthworks | A regiment's slight barricade of stones, fence rails and dirt-filled knapsacks, knee-high | E-17 | 1863-07-03 | stated | The study describes a regiment's breastwork on Cemetery Ridge as a slight barricade of stones, fence rails and knapsacks filled with dirt a little over knee-high | =It was described as a “slight barricade of stones, fence rails and knapsacks filled with dirt a little over knee-high.
114 | fences | No known stone fence along the Emmitsburg Road; stones possibly piled under the rails | FEN-emmitsburg-road | 1863-07-03 | hedged | The study says no stone fence is known along the Emmitsburg Road, though stones may have been piled two feet or so under the upper rails of the post and rail fence that paralleled the road, as was not uncommon on this part of the battlefield | =There was no known stone fence along the Emmitsburg Road. There is the possibility that the stones were piled beneath the upper most rails to the height of two feet or so, as was not uncommon on this part of the battlefield, to further strengthen the post and rail fence which paralleled the road.
120 | fences | Two fences at the Emmitsburg Road, no impediment (a soldier's account) | FEN-emmitsburg-road | 1863-07-03 | stated | A soldier's account quoted by the study says there were two fences at that road but they were no impediment | =There were two fences at that road, but they were no impediment.
128 | fences | First plank or slab fence on the Emmitsburg Road (a soldier's account) | FEN-emmitsburg-road | 1863-07-03 | stated | A soldier's account quoted by the study says the column reached the first plank or slab fence at the Emmitsburg Road and clambered over | =As the charging column neared the Emmitsburg road volley after volley of small arms aided with dreadful effect in thinning our ranks. We reached the first plank or slab fence and the column clambered over with a speed as if in stampeded retreat.
129 | fences | Sturdy post and rail fence paralleling the Emmitsburg Road slowed the charge | FEN-emmitsburg-road | 1863-07-03 | stated | The study says one officer blamed the failure of the charge on the sturdy post and rail fence paralleling the Emmitsburg Road, which slackened the impetus of the charge and led troops to take a defensive position in the sunken road | =One officer blamed the failure of the charge itself to this sturdy post and rail fence paralleling the Emmitsburg Road, because it had the effect of causing the impetus of the charge to slacken and encouraged the attacking troops to assume a defensive position in the sunken road.
129 | fences | Fences enclosing the road broke up the alignment (a soldier's account) | FEN-emmitsburg-road | 1863-07-03 | stated | An account quoted by the study says that as the troops reached the fences enclosing the road the obstruction tended greatly to break up their alignment | =As the troops in their progress reached the fences enclosing this road, the obstruction tended greatly to break up their alignment.
131 | fences | Emmitsburg Road fences near Codori's house nearly destroyed the day before | FEN-emmitsburg-road,bld-codori-farm | 1863-07-02 | stated | The passage (an account in the study) says the fences of the Emmitsburg Road near Codori's house had been nearly destroyed the day before, in the battle 'on our right' | =The fences of the Emmitsburg road, near Cordovie’s house, had been nearly destroyed the day before in the battle on our right.
